<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottoman dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1299–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as House of Osman, Ottomans, Osmanoğlu family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ottoman dynasty was the imperial House of Osman, which ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from c. 1299 to 1922. According to Ottoman tradition the family came from the Kayı branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a> in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> around Söğüt. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a> to Ahmed I, succession was often settled by violence among a dead sultan's sons, a practice known as fratricide. The sultan was usually an absolute monarch, though much power often passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>. The sultanate was abolished on 1 November 1922 and the family was sent into exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty.jpg" alt="Ottoman dynasty" width="330" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Şivekar Koçubey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rukiye_Sabiha_Sultan_wedding.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince Sabahaddin, of the <b>Ottoman dynasty</b>, fled to Europe in 1899 and with Ismail Kemal founded the Ottoman Freedom Lovers’ Committee and later the Private Enterprise and Decentralization League.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Republicanism replaced the <b>Ottoman dynasty</b> with popular sovereignty and elected government under the law: “Sovereignty belongs to the nation unrestrictedly and unconditionally.”</blockquote><a href="/ideology/kemalism">Kemalism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
